I was thinking about what it would take to replace the #enhance
system with a fully automated skill advancement system. As
much as possible of the current skill system would be kept,
including skill definitions, skill level definitions, and the skill
restriction system.

Rationale:
  - An automated skill system could reflect what the player actually
    does instead of what he wants to be good at.
  - Distribution of skills could change naturally over the course of
    the game. If I remember correctly, right now the only way to
    lose a skill level in a skill - so that you can reassign it to
another
    skill - is to lose the experience level where that skill point was
    spent.

Requirements, most important first:
  - Do not assign skill points to skills that never have been used.
  - Automatically use up all currently available skill points.
  - Automatically redistribute skill points so that frequently and
    recently used skills tend to have Expert level, while infrequently
    and not recently used skills tend to have Basic level. Any
    leftover skill points are used to raise any Skilled and Basic
skills
    toward Expert level.

Implementation:
  - Periodically decay the advance counts for skills so that
    advance points earned more recently matter more. For
    instance, decay all advance counts to 90% of their respective
    values every N turns. (Could be done after adjusting stats for
    exercise/abuse.)
  - Periodically redistribute the currently available skill points
    according to the current advance counts. (Could be done after
    decaying the advance counts.)

Comments?

